Irene bloom rad who is someone who  studies identity citizenship immigration  is going to make the case that you are  actually your citizenship and I think  she's got a broad definition of what  citizenship is Irene is a Canadian who  now resides in California where she's an  associate professor of sociology at the  University of California she studies the  nexus of immigration politics and  national identities please welcome Irene  bloom rad who will answer the question  who are you by making the case that you  are your citizenship an interesting  thing on day like today you ask  Canadians their sense of belonging and  their pride in a Canadian identity and  in being Canadian nine out of ten  roughly residents of Canada say that  they feel like they belong to Canada and  they're proud of being Canadian or a  resident of Canada now that's an  incredibly high number like think of  what would happen if nine out of ten  Canadians agreed on taxes or nine out of  ten Canadians agreed on environmental  policy and that number is even more  remarkable when we think that about one  in five Canadians was not born in this  country so one of the remarkable things  I think about that statistic is that not  only does it apply for people with  long-standing roots in this country but  it also applies to people whose parents  immigrated to this country and to people  who have themselves grown up in another  land and moved to Canada and so what I  think that does is it gives us an  opening to ask what is it about the  Canadian context that allows us to even  answer this question I am my citizenship  in a way that 9 out of 10 Canadians will  more or less agree that I am Canadian so  if you look at what constitutes Canadian  identity this is based on a pole that  the environics Institute did in 2010 you  find that health care is really  prominent that over eighty percent of  while answering this survey said that  healthcare is strongly associated with  an identity as Canadian the Charter of  Rights and Freedoms is a significant  source of identity or something that  reflects Canadian identity the flag and  the national parks and the interesting  thing about these four items that are  sort of topping the list i think is if  you cast your mind back 50 years ago and  you imagine what people might have  answered to the question of Canadian  identity 50 years ago at least three of  these four wouldn't have been there  because they didn't exist now why is  this important this is important because  when I said that these people who are  cold in these opinion polls respond to  these ways these are all residents of  Canada which as many of you probably  know includes about twenty percent of  the population of Canada that was not  born in this country so you have a very  significant foreign born population that  was born somewhere else maybe spent some  years growing up there come to Canada  and then they respond in ways that are  very similar to these responses of all  other Canadians who are maybe born in  this country why does this matter in the  context of identity over the last year  especially we've been having lots of  debates around multiculturalism and  pluralism and many of you might remember  back in October the Chancellor of  Germany and Angela Merkel she made  headlines when she pronounced that  multiculturalism had utterly failed in  Germany she was not the only one Nicolas  Sarkozy the president of France made  similar statements in just a few months  ago David Cameron the Prime Minister of  Great Britain announced or he stated  that state multiculturalism was a  problem that even if Britain you know  respected diversity  a good thing the government should not  be involved in instead should embrace a  muscular liberalism in its place and  Canada has not been immune to this The  Globe and Mail came out with a editorial  that followed up on a series that had  been doing and as you can see here the  globe's editorial said multiculturalism  should be struck from the national  vocabulary Canada needs to have the  courage to build a successful society  around the concept of citizenship a lot  of people are wondering whether the  increased diversity that comes with  immigration diversity of language of  cultures backgrounds races ethnicities  religions whether that diversity is  undermining citizenship does more  diversity mean less citizenship and  citizenship here understood as cohesion  common projects common endeavors in the  future and one of the central arguments  of my research is that policies around  pluralism diversity multiculturalism  rather than presenting that as a  zero-sum trade-off more diversity less  citizenship actually bring those two  things together and allows diversity to  become a benefit within the context of  citizenship so that we have common  citizenship now what I want to do is I  want to give you a little bit of  evidence from some research I did based  on a Canada u.s. comparison and so if  you look with using census data  comparing the proportion of immigrants  foreign-born individuals who take up  either Canadian or American citizenship  across the 20th century so across the  sweep of that century from about 1900 up  to nineteen seventy they're pretty  similar but following 1970-71 something  remarkable starts happening the canadian  numbers keep inching up more and more  immigrants take out citizenship and the  u.s. numbers dropped immigrants in  canada over the last 10 to 20 years are  much more likely to take out Canadian  citizen  than American citizenship and this  really speaks that I think the answer to  the question of identity and who we are  the Canadian context citizenship is very  very important so one of the things that  you find when you look at sort of  immigrants in the united states and  canada is immigrants in the united  states and canada both have a similar  perception of social inclusion and also  the challenges of social inclusion so in  both Canada and the United States a lot  of immigrants especially in big cities  will say you know this society tolerates  and even recognizes my background I can  be Vietnamese Canadian I can be a  Vietnamese American and so on the ground  the social reality is similar in the US  and Canada but when it comes to the  political standing of those minorities  and their ability to advance political  projects or even just to say hey I'm  here you know think about me and and and  can you please speak to some of the  issues that are dear to my heart in  Canada you have more of that  incorporation than in the United States  public policy influences our notion of  citizenship our notation of membership  and our notion of standing in a society  so what's really important I think to  highlight when you ask the core when we  when I answer the question I am my  citizenship is I am highlighting I am a  reflection of the policies of my  government and the policies of my  society that give me a sense of who I am  in that society and that sort of bundled  together under the label of citizenship  the other reason that it's important  though is that I think citizenship  especially in democratic countries is  extremely important to give people the  sense of power that they can make  changes to their society and citizenship  is an important way for us to mobilize  identities to do things in the future  fundamentally integration into a society  is about the social connections we have  those social interactions and it can be  with organizations it can be with  community leaders churches religious  organizations etc they facilitate that  process of citizenship and so I'm going  to give you some examples of that in a  second that infrastructure the support  of infrastructure of community  organizations is fundamentally helped  along by things such as settlement  policy integration policy  multiculturalism policies so the policy  infrastructure provides a foundation  upon which immigrant communities can  mobilize themselves they can do their  own things they do their own stuff but  especially for immigrant communities  that might not have the same resources  might not come with a lot of financial  capital might not come with a lot of  education because where they came from  they didn't really have the opportunity  to go very far they really need sort of  this seed money let's say to help them  get integrated and also get on the path  of citizenship and then finally the  final point I'd like to make is that  public policy it's important for  instrumental reasons having a room  available for people to meet so that  they can talk about something in the  local library is fundamentally important  for helping this process along but it's  also the symbolic notions behind  policies the policies in place obviously  do things they give money they  you know they do technical things they  set up regulations but those policies  also in the minds of people represent  things and if you want additional  evidence beyond the immigrant case just  think of that first or the second  statistic I showed you about healthcare  you know who would have thought that  someone's identity as a Canadian would  be linked to whether you have health  insurance or not but clearly that is the  case in Canada so policies carry these  identity components with them which then  builds this bundle of citizenship I  interviewed Doris Meissner who was the  Commissioner for the immigration and  naturalization service when that still  existed on with the Clinton  administration she is probably one of  the inas commissioners who were most  dedicated to citizenship she wanted to  put the end back in i ns she was going  to put naturalization at the forefront  it didn't work out that way she actually  started the increase in the border  control in the United States and when  she reflected back and her time as an AI  NS Commissioner she said the dominant  culture of the agency is rooted in a  view of immigration as a source of  security and law enforcement  vulnerability more than of continued  nation-building currently in the United  States immigration and naturalization  services are within the Department of  Homeland Security and just by the name  of that agency you can imagine that that  hasn't really changed in the Canadian  case I interviewed some senior officials  at Citizenship and Immigration I took a  sort of standard American line and said  why are using taxpayer dollars to do all  of this citizenship stuff and the woman  I spoke to got quite upset with me and  said why would we not encourage  citizenship we say everyone belongs that  we believe in diversity when we promote  citizenship let's be clear it talks  about applying yes but at its beginning  it's about integration and this kind of  attitude immigrants get this but they  understand what's going on so with an  interview that I did with a Portuguese  American  who is low income in the Boston area she  was complaining about rents and she said  but they the government don't know how  it is you can't say sir Sir send the  people that care about the immigrants  because they are the people who can't  speak up there is nowhere to complain to  and we can't complain so very much a  feeling of being shut out in this other  case Joe was a really interesting person  because he had deep attachments to  Portugal and he actually held both  Canadian and American citizenship and I  asked him what you know who are you I  guess I asked him who are you you know  what are you and he said I'm Canadian  and I said well why are you Canadian why  not these other things he said well I  like the way the government runs things  I guess the great support the  multi-culture stuff the social  assistance we pay a lot of tax that's  true but the great concern of government  the help for the citizens themselves and  so he felt much more included in  government and in the citizenry so when  you know you can imagine Joe having to  answer this question about do you feel  you belong and he would be one of those  nine out of ten residents of Canada and  say yeah I do feel like I belong  you know fundamentally my research is  saying that public policies matter when  it comes to immigrant incorporation into  political systems and into citizenship  and that those public policies have to  provide both instrumental supports to  immigrants so that they can become part  of society and be active in civil  society and it has to provide a sense of  symbolic citizenship and a sense of  symbolic openness to who they are and  where they come from and in countries  that are able to bring both support to  immigrant communities and settlement  assistance together with this message  that we recognize and we appreciate you  in those countries immigrants are more  likely to participate and more likely to  be part of the things that we think of a  citizenship in terms of participating in  local schools running for office getting  together with others to solve problems  and even taking to the streets to  protest when they don't like what's  going on if you look at perceived  discrimination the unitary countries  those are countries that really try to  have a unitary notion of belonging  immigrants in those countries are more  likely to perceive discrimination so  they're more likely to see  discrimination visiter themselves  compared to the pluralist places the  places that have more diversity policies  more open citizenship and if you ask  about political trust trust in political  institutions trust in politicians the  ones who are in the more pluralist  societies are more likely to feel a  little bit more likely to feel trust in  those institutions and politicians than  the ones in the more unitary societies  or the societies where the notion of  citizenship is very unitary but I was  also argued to get back to the very  first point public policy really matters  in constituting our identities and in  the Canadian case it's an identity that  does have important links to public  policies not only multiculturalism but  things like health care national parks  etc and then the answer to who we are is  we are citizenship thank you very much  you
 

